{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "What is the objective of the scheduling plan — are we minimizing the total weekly labor cost (sum of wage × hours for all students), or is there another goal such as maximizing fairness or minimizing total hours worked?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the optimization objective, presenting alternatives within one coherent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "does this mean the lab is open all 14 hours every day from Monday through Friday, and the \"maximum duty hours\" in Table 5-9 are the upper bounds on how many of those 14 hours each student can work on that day?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for confirmation of a single combined interpretation regarding the lab's operating schedule and the definition of the maximum duty hours constraint."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "are students 1–6 all undergraduates, or is there a split between undergraduate and graduate students, and if so, which IDs are which?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single classification of the student IDs into undergraduate or graduate categories. Although it includes a conditional clause ('if so, which IDs are which'), it is part of the same request to determine the status of the specific students, not a separate independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Since the undergraduate/graduate split is still being confirmed internally, can you tell me which students are undergraduates and which are graduates, or should I treat all six students as the same category for now and apply a single minimum-hours rule?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response presents a single decision point: either provide the specific split or confirm treating all students as one category. These are mutually exclusive options for the same underlying issue, constituting one independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Setting aside the undergraduate/graduate split for now, is there any other constraint on how many hours a single student can work in total across the whole week, or is the only weekly limit the minimum (at least 8 or 7 hours) with the daily caps from Table 5-9 being the only upper bounds?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking whether there are additional weekly hour constraints beyond the specified minimums and daily caps."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "You mentioned a limit of no more than 2 shifts per week — does \"shift\" here mean a single continuous block of hours worked on one day (so a student could work up to 2 days per week), or does it mean something else like a fixed-length time slot?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single definition of the term 'shift' to clarify the constraint, which constitutes one semantically independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Since each student can work on at most 2 days per week, and the daily caps in Table 5-9 already show 0 for some day-student combinations (meaning they cannot work that day), is the \"at most 2 days per week\" constraint an additional restriction on top of the table's zeros, or does it replace them — that is, could a student with a non-zero cap on 3 or more days still only pick 2 of those days?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for the relationship between the weekly day limit and the daily caps, specifically whether the limit is additive or if it restricts selection among available days."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "When a student works on a given day, must they work a single continuous block of hours (e.g., 9:00 AM to 3:00 PM), or can they work multiple separate blocks within that day as long as the total doesn't exceed the daily cap?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the continuity of work hours, presenting two mutually exclusive options for the user to choose from."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "can you tell me whether the lab's 14 open hours (8:00 AM to 10:00 PM) need to be divided into fixed time slots (e.g., 2-hour blocks), or is the scheduling done at the level of whole hours where any student can be assigned to any hour as long as exactly one student covers each hour?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the granularity of scheduling (fixed blocks vs. whole hours), which constitutes one semantically independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "For the purpose of building the model, is it acceptable to assume that the 14 hours each day are divided into 14 one-hour slots, and a student assigned to a day works a single continuous block of consecutive hours (so if they work 5 hours on Monday, it would be, say, 8-13 or 10-15, not scattered hours)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for confirmation of a specific modeling assumption regarding time slots and continuity."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Given that the continuous-block detail is still pending, for now can I proceed with the simpler assumption that a student assigned to a day simply works some number of hours (up to the daily cap) and we don't need to track which specific hours they cover, as long as the total hours across all students equals 14 each day?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification request asking for permission to adopt a specific simplifying assumption regarding hour tracking."
}

